<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
PrivacyFence *companion* autostart Task Scheduler task definition
(#428 Phase 4, B5c).

scripts/windows_privilege_separation.ps1 substitutes __EXEC_PATH__ with the
real installed PrivacyFenceCompanion.exe path and registers it via
`schtasks /create /tn "PrivacyFenceCompanion" /xml <file> /f`. It is written
only by that script's `enable`, never by the installer: an unseparated
install autostarts the daemon in the user's own session exactly as it always
has, and the companion stays opt-in there.

This is ADR 0002's "startup wiring inverts" on Windows. What used to start
at sign-in was the daemon (installer/privacyfence-task.xml.tmpl, still
registered by the installer but disabled by `enable`); what starts at
sign-in now is the companion, while the daemon has become a service running
as NT SERVICE\PrivacyFence. On this platform the companion is not merely a
convenience: a service runs in session 0, so the daemon can no longer call
webbrowser.open() for a Slack/Salesforce/Atlassian OAuth flow, and the
companion's own control channel is what opens those pages instead
(ADR 0002 decision 5, the case that decision was originally written for).

Structure deliberately mirrors privacyfence-task.xml.tmpl: same schema
version, same Principal id / Actions Context pairing, same StartBoundary
convention, same two inverted battery defaults, and the same
no-encoding-attribute XML declaration. Every one of those is load bearing
for the reasons that file's own header comment spells out at length; this
one does not repeat them.

Three things differ from the daemon's task, and each one is deliberate:

  * No TimeTrigger. The daemon's task carries a repeating PT5M trigger as
    its real crash-restart mechanism, and relies on the single-instance lock
    (privacyfence.daemon_main._acquire_instance_lock) to make every
    redundant tick exit immediately. The companion has no such lock, so a
    repeating trigger would stack up one tray icon per tick. Crash restart
    for the *daemon* is now the service's own `sc failure` configuration,
    which is a better mechanism than the one this replaces; for the
    companion there is no automatic restart, and the Start Menu shortcut the
    installer creates is the manual one.

  * MultipleInstancesPolicy is IgnoreNew, not Parallel. Same reason: a
    second tray icon for the same session is never wanted. It stays correct
    for multiple simultaneously signed-in users, which is what the daemon's
    task needed Parallel for, because Task Scheduler scopes that policy per
    task instance and each user's logon produces its own.

  * RestartOnFailure stays, unchanged, for the one narrow thing it does: a
    launch failure right at the LogonTrigger's own moment (a locked file
    mid-upgrade) is retried three times a minute apart rather than leaving
    the user with no way into the web UI until their next sign-in.

LogonTrigger with no UserId fires for any interactive logon, and Principal
uses GroupId (the built-in Users group) rather than UserId, so the companion
runs as whichever user just signed in, in their own session, which is the
whole point of it. It must be LeastPrivilege: this process is deliberately
on the *agent's* side of the trust boundary, not the daemon's.

NOTE: this comment intentionally avoids the two-hyphen sequence, which XML
comments may not contain anywhere in their body.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>Starts the PrivacyFence companion (tray icon and browser-opening channel) when any user signs in.</Description>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2020-01-01T00:00:00</StartBoundary>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <GroupId>Builtin\Users</GroupId>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>3</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>__EXEC_PATH__</Command>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
